---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Index from '../components/Index.astro';
import Hazard from '../components/Hazard.astro';
---

<Base
  title="What we never collect"
  description="One analytics script on the website, nothing at all in the tools, and a list of exactly what each one receives."
  status="draft"
  here="/privacy"
>
  <section class="band band--first">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={1} of={5} />
      <h2 class="band__title">The short version</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">
      The extension and both command line tools make no network requests. This website loads one
      script, from Cloudflare, which counts page views. That is the whole list.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={2} of={5} />
      <h2 class="band__title">The tools</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Everything the extension reads stays in your browser's own storage. Everything the command
        line tools read is already on your disk, in transcript files the tool you were using wrote
        there. Neither sends anything anywhere, and neither needs an account.
      </p>
      <p>
        That claim is checked rather than asserted. A test in the repository reads the built
        extension files and fails if any way of reaching the network appears in them that is not on
        a short list with a written reason beside it. Today that list has two entries: reading the
        packaged tokenizer table out of the extension's own files, and the browser's own script
        loader reading our interceptor out of the extension on Firefox. Both addresses belong to the
        extension.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prompt text is never stored. Where the extension needs to tell two exchanges apart it keeps
        a hash, not the words.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={3} of={5} />
      <h2 class="band__title">This website</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>One script loads, and here it is in full:</p>
      <pre><code>https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js</code></pre>
      <p>
        It is Cloudflare Web Analytics, added by hand rather than injected by the hosting platform,
        so that this paragraph can stay true. It posts to <code>/cdn-cgi/rum</code> on this domain.
        Cloudflare receives the page address, the referrer, your browser's user agent string, the
        country it infers from your address, and some page timings. It sets no cookie, and
        Cloudflare state that they do not fingerprint visitors or build profiles across sites.
      </p>
      <p>
        Block it and the site works exactly as it does now. Nothing on any page depends on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fonts are served from this domain, not from a font service, so no third party learns
        which pages you read. There are no forms, no comments, no embedded video and no cookies.
      </p>
      <p>
        The calculator keeps its state in the part of the address after the <code>#</code>. Browsers
        never send that part to a server, which is why it is there rather than in a query string.
        Your figures stay on your screen even when you copy the link and paste it to somebody.
      </p>
      <p>
        One key goes in local storage, <code>buoa-theme</code>, holding the word light or dark. It
        never leaves the browser it was set in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={4} of={5} />
      <h2 class="band__title">Logs at the edge</h2>
    </div>
    <Hazard label="The one thing we cannot switch off">
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0">
        The site is served by Cloudflare, so Cloudflare sees the requests that fetch it, the way any
        host sees the requests it serves. We do not run a server, we cannot read those logs, and we
        have no way to connect them to anything. It is still worth saying out loud, because a
        privacy page that claims nobody anywhere sees anything would be wrong.
      </p>
    </Hazard>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={5} of={5} />
      <h2 class="band__title">If this changes</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Any change to this page arrives as a commit in the public repository, and the{' '}
        <a href="/changelog">changelog</a> records it. If a future release ever needed to send
        something, it would ask first and the answer could be no.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
